WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:06.620 --> 00:00:16.790
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Perhaps now we can move to
the next agenda item, which is the new Cancer

00:00:16.790 --> 00:00:22.259
Drugs Fund. This has been here before. It
is not new news, but we have had the consultation,

00:00:22.259 --> 00:00:26.640
and therefore I think the purpose of this
is to review that consultation and then to

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:33.030
agree the specific way forward, in order that
we can give clarity to all stakeholders. Bruce,

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:34.950
I think you are providing the intro on this.

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:40.550
&gt;&gt;Bruce Keogh: Thanks very much, Chair. I
will just start by giving a little bit of

00:00:40.550 --> 00:00:48.570
context. Improving cancer care is an absolute
priority for NHS England, and the reason for

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:55.159
that is that it touches the lives and families
of so many citizens of this country.

00:00:55.159 --> 00:01:01.739
Globally and in the NHS, we have made a lot
of progress over the last decade, but that

00:01:01.739 --> 00:01:07.540
is dependent on access to the newest treatments
and the most effective treatments, as quickly

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:09.100
as possible.

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:16.570
So improving the Cancer Drugs Fund is one
of our commitments. We have discussed this

00:01:16.570 --> 00:01:21.640
at the Board in 2015 and, indeed, since then
we have had a National Audit Office report,

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:29.880
a Public Accounts Committee report and a Cancer
Taskforce led by Harpal Kumar, all of which

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:33.570
suggested that we needed to improve the Cancer
Drugs Fund.

00:01:33.570 --> 00:01:39.920
So in late 2014 I started discussions with
the charities, the Association of British

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:46.640
Pharmaceutical Industries, the Department
of Health, NICE, the MHRA and others so that

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:52.980
we could start to think of a way forward for
the Cancer Drugs Fund.

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:58.650
We got to a point of formal consultation in
November 2015, and that consultation closed

00:01:58.650 --> 00:02:08.170
on 11 February. During that time we had 286
responses, variably from the pharmaceutical

00:02:08.170 --> 00:02:13.920
industry, patient groups and charities, people
working in the National Health Service and

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:15.290
individuals.

00:02:15.290 --> 00:02:24.499
Of course, we need time to fully process some
of those, but there were three main themes

00:02:24.499 --> 00:02:28.799
that seem to have emerged from the consultation.
The first is that there is general support

00:02:28.799 --> 00:02:36.310
for a managed access process with clear entry
and exit criteria into the Cancer Drugs Fund.

00:02:36.310 --> 00:02:42.459
The second is that there was a need for much
greater clarity on the operational detail,

00:02:42.459 --> 00:02:49.299
and in particular for the mechanisms of financial
control. Thirdly, people wanted to be reassured

00:02:49.299 --> 00:02:55.909
that we had clear details of transition to
the new Cancer Drugs Fund.

00:02:55.909 --> 00:03:02.790
Interestingly, and not surprisingly, there
were some contrasting views. People asked

00:03:02.790 --> 00:03:07.719
whether the new Cancer Drugs Fund would lead
to more or less access to cancer drugs, and

00:03:07.719 --> 00:03:14.010
I will come back to that. There were also
questions raised about whether cancer should

00:03:14.010 --> 00:03:20.019
have a special status, over and above treatments
for other conditions.

00:03:20.019 --> 00:03:27.370
The paper which is in front of you describes
our response to the consultation. I think

00:03:27.370 --> 00:03:34.409
I would like to start by drawing people's
attention to the front page of the paper,

00:03:34.409 --> 00:03:40.099
where we quite clearly state that, for patients,
the new, improved version of the Cancer Drugs

00:03:40.099 --> 00:03:46.159
Fund will help provide faster access to the
most promising cancer treatments for our patients.

00:03:46.159 --> 00:03:52.620
Secondly, for taxpayers, the new Cancer Drugs
Fund will drive stronger value for money in

00:03:52.620 --> 00:04:02.439
drugs expenditure. Finally, for drug companies
who are willing to price their products appropriately,

00:04:02.439 --> 00:04:09.439
the Cancer Drugs Fund offers a new fast-track
route to NHS funding for drugs at the point

00:04:09.439 --> 00:04:14.079
of market authorisation.

00:04:14.079 --> 00:04:20.530
The key elements of a managed access process
are these, and I will go through them at a

00:04:20.530 --> 00:04:25.949
relatively high level. Firstly, all new cancer
drugs will be referred to NICE for assessment.

00:04:25.949 --> 00:04:29.150
This is a new development.

00:04:29.150 --> 00:04:36.050
Secondly, the assessment will not simply be
yes or no; there will be an option for a maybe

00:04:36.050 --> 00:04:42.460
when there is some level of uncertainty about
the value proposition or effectiveness of

00:04:42.460 --> 00:04:46.060
a given drug.

00:04:46.060 --> 00:04:53.349
Those drugs or indications which are regarded
as a maybe will become candidates for the

00:04:53.349 --> 00:05:00.860
new Cancer Drugs Fund, and entry into that
will be approved by a joint NHS/NICE investment

00:05:00.860 --> 00:05:08.120
group or committee. Once a drug has spent
time in the Cancer Drugs Fund and the uncertainty

00:05:08.120 --> 00:05:16.129
has been resolved or enough data has been
collected, NICE will perform a final appraisal

00:05:16.129 --> 00:05:23.310
and opine yes or no with respect to whether
that drug should move into routine commissioning.

00:05:23.310 --> 00:05:28.300
The end result of this means that flow through
the Cancer Drugs Fund, because there are clear

00:05:28.300 --> 00:05:38.629
entry and exit criteria, will be greater and
more flexible than it is at the moment. However,

00:05:38.629 --> 00:05:45.389
this does require NICE to work at a greater
speed than it has done in the past.

00:05:45.389 --> 00:05:51.720
To that end, it will produce draft guidance
on drugs before marketing authorisation, with

00:05:51.720 --> 00:06:00.599
the aim of producing final guidance within
90 days of marketing authorisation. That will

00:06:00.599 --> 00:06:06.750
require additional resourcing, and we have
received assurance from NICE that they will

00:06:06.750 --> 00:06:15.000
be able to develop the capacity and capability
to meet those particular challenges.

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:24.050
The new Cancer Drugs Fund will go live on
1st July this year, not on 1st April. This

00:06:24.050 --> 00:06:33.729
will give us time to resolve a number of issues.
Firstly, the practical aspects of NICE publishing

00:06:33.729 --> 00:06:37.909
initial recommendations prior to marketing
authorisation.

00:06:37.909 --> 00:06:46.650
Secondly, to enable us to address the risk
of funding ineffective treatments during transition.

00:06:46.650 --> 00:06:55.090
Thirdly, how we can treat rare cancers and
off-label drugs fairly. It is likely that

00:06:55.090 --> 00:07:03.069
those particular drug and indication combinations
will be associated with greater uncertainty,

00:07:03.069 --> 00:07:08.050
so that is likely to give them much greater
access into the Cancer Drugs Fund, which is

00:07:08.050 --> 00:07:12.050
different from the position that we find ourselves
in at the moment where they are often crowded

00:07:12.050 --> 00:07:17.259
out by more expensive drugs for more common
conditions.

00:07:17.259 --> 00:07:27.050
Finally, it will give us time to resolve the
issue of whether we simply keep drugs in the

00:07:27.050 --> 00:07:35.020
fund long enough to resolve the uncertainty,
or whether we aim to keep drugs in the fund

00:07:35.020 --> 00:07:40.409
in a way that gives maximum access to maximum
patients. I would refer people to the third

00:07:40.409 --> 00:07:46.939
bullet point at paragraph 17 for that particular
issue.

00:07:46.939 --> 00:07:54.699
So on 1 April this year we will close the
current Cancer Drugs Fund, but roll drugs

00:07:54.699 --> 00:08:02.449
over. Drugs that are in the Cancer Drugs Fund
will remain in it until they have been through

00:08:02.449 --> 00:08:13.930
a final appraisal process by NICE. We also
need to sort out transitional funding arrangements.

00:08:13.930 --> 00:08:19.439
Those will be delegated, as you will see towards
the end of the paper, to the Chief Executive,

00:08:19.439 --> 00:08:27.860
the Chief Financial Officer and myself. We
are clear that we will anticipate seeing reimbursement

00:08:27.860 --> 00:08:52.779
for these drugs 
at or below the rates which are in place in
March 2016. We also need to ensure that we

00:08:52.779 --> 00:08:59.560
keep the new Cancer Drugs Fund within a fixed
budget of £340 million.

00:08:59.560 --> 00:09:07.260
The methodology for controlling that expenditure
has been and may be described by the Chief

00:09:07.260 --> 00:09:14.500
Financial Officer. So it seems to me that
the new operating model will remove some level

00:09:14.500 --> 00:09:17.010
of uncertainty which currently exists.

00:09:17.010 --> 00:09:22.810
It will ensure that we are able to balance
the budget, and it will give patients clarity

00:09:22.810 --> 00:09:28.779
about the status of any given drug or drug
combinations much sooner than the current

00:09:28.779 --> 00:09:30.920
Cancer Drugs Fund does. Thank you.

00:09:30.920 --> 00:09:34.149
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Simon, anything you want
to add to this?

00:09:34.149 --> 00:09:40.029
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: No. I think Bruce summarised
it well. This is the substance of the discussion

00:09:40.029 --> 00:09:44.490
we had very extensively prior to going out
to consultation, with some important adjustments

00:09:44.490 --> 00:09:46.750
made in the light of consultation.

00:09:46.750 --> 00:09:49.990
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Okay, great. So, questions.
Victor.

00:09:49.990 --> 00:09:57.620
&gt;&gt;Victor Adebowale: Just to be clear, will
every patient getting drugs under the current

00:09:57.620 --> 00:10:03.329
fund continue to get drugs under the new regime?

00:10:03.329 --> 00:10:08.810
&gt;&gt;Bruce Keogh: Just to be clear, the answer
is yes.

00:10:08.810 --> 00:10:09.630
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Unequivocally?

00:10:09.630 --> 00:10:10.730
&gt;&gt;Bruce Keogh: Absolutely, Chair.

00:10:10.730 --> 00:10:12.100
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Good. Okay. John.

00:10:12.100 --> 00:10:15.630
&gt;&gt;John Burn: We all knew, when the Cancer
Drugs Fund was introduced that it was something

00:10:15.630 --> 00:10:21.160
of an emergency measure and an emergency treatment,
so this is actually a really impressive adaptation

00:10:21.160 --> 00:10:27.510
and evolution of that into something that
is much more adaptable to the needs of now.

00:10:27.510 --> 00:10:31.300
You said at the beginning that one of the
criticisms was that it still has the word

00:10:31.300 --> 00:10:36.839
'cancer' on the front, and it is only one
class of disease, albeit one of our most pressing

00:10:36.839 --> 00:10:41.300
ones. Noel and I sit on the accelerated access
review, which is looking at the broader picture

00:10:41.300 --> 00:10:45.480
of how we get new, innovative treatments into
the Health Service.

00:10:45.480 --> 00:10:52.199
Can you just maybe connect those two together?
How does the accelerated access review connect

00:10:52.199 --> 00:10:56.899
to this process? Is this informing that process,
or will that process inform the further evolution

00:10:56.899 --> 00:10:58.510
of the Cancer Drugs Fund?

00:10:58.510 --> 00:11:04.699
&gt;&gt;Bruce Keogh: I think the Cancer Drugs Fund
will inform the accelerated access review.

00:11:04.699 --> 00:11:12.649
In May last year, I wrote to George Freeman
indicating that we needed to link the two

00:11:12.649 --> 00:11:13.870
processes.

00:11:13.870 --> 00:11:21.120
I think the issue which will influence that
is the speed at which the accelerated access

00:11:21.120 --> 00:11:28.639
review can come to its final conclusions,
but it is quite clear from their interim report

00:11:28.639 --> 00:11:30.899
that the two are pretty well aligned.

00:11:32.360 --> 00:11:37.740
&gt;&gt;Noel Gordon: May I just say in passing,
Chairman, that this paper came through specialised

00:11:37.740 --> 00:11:44.389
commissioning yesterday? Or course Sir John,
who is a real cancer expert, Margaret Casely

00:11:44.389 --> 00:11:50.839
Hayford and myself put it under the expected
challenge and test of an assurance process.

00:11:50.839 --> 00:11:56.019
So I wanted to make the point that this has
already, in a sense, been battle-tested as

00:11:56.019 --> 00:11:58.740
far as our assurance process is concerned.

00:11:58.740 --> 00:12:09.259
Bruce, the CDF as it is currently constituted
worked for what I might call common conditions.

00:12:09.259 --> 00:12:16.610
How is it going to help with patients suffering
from rarer cancer conditions?

00:12:16.610 --> 00:12:22.459
&gt;&gt;Bruce Keogh: The way I see it working is
that the new Cancer Drugs Fund is largely

00:12:22.459 --> 00:12:32.980
there to resolve issues of uncertainty related
to the effectiveness of different drugs. In

00:12:32.980 --> 00:12:38.170
the past, drugs for rare cancers have not
been appraised by NICE.

00:12:38.170 --> 00:12:42.440
They will be appraised by NICE as part of
the new process. I think that will expose

00:12:42.440 --> 00:12:47.720
that, in many cases, there are degrees of
uncertainty, which I think is likely to lead

00:12:47.720 --> 00:12:54.389
to much greater and easier access for those
particular drugs into the Cancer Drugs Fund.

00:12:54.389 --> 00:13:07.389
&gt;&gt;Moira Gibb: Bruce, this is not simple. It
is often talked about in very simplistic terms,

00:13:07.389 --> 00:13:14.910
though, in the media, so I would like something
very simple: how is this better for patients

00:13:14.910 --> 00:13:16.980
than what we have now?

00:13:16.980 --> 00:13:26.240
&gt;&gt;Bruce Keogh: I think it will provide faster
access for patients, and I think that is really

00:13:26.240 --> 00:13:34.660
important. I think it will also bring clarity
as to which drugs are the most effective drugs,

00:13:34.660 --> 00:13:40.399
sooner than we know at the moment. I think
those are probably the two main things.

00:13:40.399 --> 00:13:43.300
&gt;&gt;Moira Gibb: Thank you.

00:13:43.300 --> 00:13:51.819
&gt;&gt;Bruce Keogh: May I just add one other point
that is quite important here? The current

00:13:51.819 --> 00:13:59.250
Cancer Drugs Fund is full. What we have with
the new Cancer Drugs Fund is a system that

00:13:59.250 --> 00:14:02.250
is much more fluid and much more flexible.

00:14:02.250 --> 00:14:12.399
We have clear entry and exit criteria, so
we can bring new drugs in with greater ease,

00:14:12.399 --> 00:14:17.269
if you like, because we can get ineffective
drugs out, or drugs where the uncertainty

00:14:17.269 --> 00:14:22.440
is resolved, or drugs which move into routine
commissioning earlier, in a way that we cannot

00:14:22.440 --> 00:14:25.709
do quite as easily at the moment.

00:14:25.709 --> 00:14:32.910
&gt;&gt;Noel Gordon: Bruce, you mentioned in your
introduction the consultation process and

00:14:32.910 --> 00:14:35.360
the 286 responses received.

00:14:35.360 --> 00:14:41.759
I was interested, as I was reading through
the additional papers that you provided here,

00:14:41.759 --> 00:14:50.079
that of the overall number of respondents
something like 34% declared having received

00:14:50.079 --> 00:14:57.610
payment from drug companies and, a sub-category
within that overall total being patients'

00:14:57.610 --> 00:15:06.060
and voluntary groups, 81% declared they had
received drug company funding.

00:15:06.060 --> 00:15:11.970
Does that concern you in any way, particularly
with reference to our sunlight disclosure

00:15:11.970 --> 00:15:12.339
programme?

00:15:12.339 --> 00:15:19.029
&gt;&gt;Bruce Keogh: I think there are two things
about that. Firstly, you would expect a number

00:15:19.029 --> 00:15:26.050
of the respondents to have interests in the
pharmaceutical industry, but the second thing

00:15:26.050 --> 00:15:31.800
- and this is the most important point - is
that people have declared it.

00:15:31.800 --> 00:15:38.440
I think that is really important, because
that brings clarity to any potential conflicts

00:15:38.440 --> 00:15:43.300
of interest or views that people might express.

00:15:43.300 --> 00:15:52.339
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: A couple of questions from
me, if I may, Bruce. There was some suggestion

00:15:52.339 --> 00:15:58.800
that a number of drugs - quite a significant
proportion of the drugs - currently in the

00:15:58.800 --> 00:16:06.750
CDF will not make it through the NICE evaluation
or make it into routine commissioning.

00:16:06.750 --> 00:16:14.389
I just wonder what we think about that, and
whether you would agree with some of the external

00:16:14.389 --> 00:16:19.420
reporting that has been done on this?

00:16:19.420 --> 00:16:26.129
&gt;&gt;Bruce Keogh: We function in a tax-funded
National Health Service. We have an obligation,

00:16:26.129 --> 00:16:31.610
I think, to get the best value for money for
taxpayers, who are also our patients.

00:16:31.610 --> 00:16:36.730
It is quite a - I was going to say it is a
simple formula, but it is not. The formula

00:16:36.730 --> 00:16:43.759
which is applied is: how effective is a drug,
and how much does it cost? Now, the efficacy

00:16:43.759 --> 00:16:44.980
is pretty fixed.

00:16:44.980 --> 00:16:49.910
There is one component of that formula which
is under the control of the pharmaceutical

00:16:49.910 --> 00:16:56.670
companies, and that is the cost of the drug.
By influencing the cost of the drug, you can

00:16:56.670 --> 00:17:01.970
influence the likelihood of that drug entering
routine commissioning.

00:17:01.970 --> 00:17:07.560
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: So we do not go into this
with any sort of firm, fixed view on this?

00:17:07.560 --> 00:17:12.510
These are things that are still open for discussion
at an appropriate time?

00:17:12.510 --> 00:17:13.970
&gt;&gt;Bruce Keogh: Exactly.

00:17:13.970 --> 00:17:20.860
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Good. Then the second thing
is, within the consultation as I read it,

00:17:20.860 --> 00:17:31.950
one of the most significant areas of concern
is about the capacity and timeliness within

00:17:31.950 --> 00:17:40.090
NICE to be able to do these reviews, which
are intense, time-consuming, difficult and

00:17:40.090 --> 00:17:40.130
so on.

00:17:40.130 --> 00:17:45.450
I think it would be appropriate for us to
understand what assurances and what perspective

00:17:45.450 --> 00:17:50.080
we may have on that, given it was one of the
most important aspects of the consultation?

00:17:50.080 --> 00:17:53.659
&gt;&gt;Bruce Keogh: Well, the first thing I would
say is I think it has been a very legitimate

00:17:53.659 --> 00:17:58.700
concern that people have expressed, and that
will have come from many different quarters,

00:17:58.700 --> 00:18:03.409
not least of all patients who have to wait.

00:18:03.409 --> 00:18:11.039
So there are two things. Firstly, NICE needs
to do more work and have greater capability

00:18:11.039 --> 00:18:16.659
and capacity, and that requires resourcing,
which Paul may want to say something about.

00:18:16.659 --> 00:18:21.890
However, the second thing - and this is the
important thing - is that we have received

00:18:21.890 --> 00:18:28.520
assurance from NICE that, with the appropriate
resourcing, they can put that capability and

00:18:28.520 --> 00:18:29.690
capacity in place.

00:18:29.690 --> 00:18:34.720
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: I understand their board
is discussing this fairly soon, is that right?

00:18:34.720 --> 00:18:36.480
&gt;&gt;Bruce Keogh: Yes. I think it is 17th March.

00:18:36.480 --> 00:18:42.720
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Okay. Paul, you were nicely
set up by your friend. Is there anything that

00:18:42.720 --> 00:18:43.679
you would like to add?

00:18:43.679 --> 00:18:47.429
&gt;&gt;Paul Baumann: Set up as usual. I think the
assurances from NICE are the important part

00:18:47.429 --> 00:18:53.280
of that. They have shared with us their very
specific resourcing plans around this, which

00:18:53.280 --> 00:18:57.820
obviously are predicated on the forward pipeline
that we can see, and indeed the transitional

00:18:57.820 --> 00:18:59.380
pipeline that we have.

00:18:59.380 --> 00:19:03.830
So I am very comfortable that they have considered
the correct volume of drugs, as far as we

00:19:03.830 --> 00:19:07.500
can see that. They have worked out what the
resource is that they need to live to the

00:19:07.500 --> 00:19:12.600
standards that have been set in this. So I
see no reason for the Board to doubt the assurances

00:19:12.600 --> 00:19:14.100
that we have been given.

00:19:14.100 --> 00:19:18.230
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Therefore, Bruce, from a
clinical perspective you are comfortable that

00:19:18.230 --> 00:19:22.690
this is the best solution for the way forward,
for patients in particular?

00:19:22.690 --> 00:19:30.110
&gt;&gt;Bruce Keogh: Yes, I am. I think this has
been a comprehensive, complex process. Some

00:19:30.110 --> 00:19:38.570
of the discussions have inevitably been difficult.
However, I think there is a consensus view,

00:19:38.570 --> 00:19:44.600
certainly among clinical colleagues, that
we have moved the dial and moved things forward.

00:19:44.600 --> 00:19:48.720
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Paul, you are comfortable
from a financial perspective as well that

00:19:48.720 --> 00:19:50.880
this is the best alternative we can come up
with?

00:19:50.880 --> 00:19:52.080
&gt;&gt;Paul Baumann: It is.

00:19:52.080 --> 00:19:56.659
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: You are comfortable, Noel,
that from SpecCom yesterday you gave this

00:19:56.659 --> 00:20:01.789
a good working over, and the consultation
has been appropriately considered?

00:20:01.789 --> 00:20:06.990
&gt;&gt;Noel Gordon: Yes. From both a design and
implementation perspective, I think we performed

00:20:06.990 --> 00:20:09.510
a pretty rigorous assurance process yesterday.

00:20:09.510 --> 00:20:16.960
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Okay. Any other final questions
or comments? Simon, anything you would add?

00:20:16.960 --> 00:20:23.380
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: No. Just to underline, we
chartered an independent cancer taskforce

00:20:23.380 --> 00:20:28.580
led by Sir Harpal Kumar, the chief executive
of Cancer Research UK. Their clear recommendation

00:20:28.580 --> 00:20:33.150
to us was to do what we are proposing this
afternoon.

00:20:33.150 --> 00:20:41.960
Secondly, we have had a National Audit Office
review of the Cancer Drugs Fund Mark I, and

00:20:41.960 --> 00:20:48.429
it clearly draws attention to the need to
change the way the CDF works. Thirdly, that

00:20:48.429 --> 00:20:55.650
case was not only supported but accentuated
by the Public Accounts Committee of the House

00:20:55.650 --> 00:20:58.690
of Commons, who produced a report to that
effect as well.

00:20:58.690 --> 00:21:05.250
So I think a combination of our cancer taskforce,
the National Audit Office in respect of the

00:21:05.250 --> 00:21:11.789
good use of public resources, and Parliament
through the PAC: this is going with the grain

00:21:11.789 --> 00:21:16.210
of what people see as the sensible evolution
of the Cancer Drugs Fund.

00:21:16.210 --> 00:21:26.409
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Thank you. So now, I think,
if there are no other questions or comments,

